# harness-init

Blueprint: `docs/planning/research/07-autoconfig-patterns.md` (the
understand-anything pattern — SCAN is always a deterministic script, the LLM only decides/
narrates on top of the JSON the script produces; never reimplement the detection
logic in prose). Decision D5 (`docs/planning/00-plano-consolidado.md` §11):
the framework installs EVERYTHING, but each stack-specific component is classified
by a deterministic RULE, never by the model's "guesswork" — and nothing CONDICIONAL
is enabled without explicit human confirmation.

Engine: `.harness/lib/scan_project.py` (scan/classify/memory-surfaces/answers)
+ `.harness/lib/merge_docs.py` (additive merge). You (the agent running this
skill) only orchestrate calls to these two scripts and drive the conversation with the
user — you never decide applicability "by eye".

## ARGS

```text
AGENT=claude | codex | both   (default: both — controls copier's use_claude/use_codex)
MODE=install | update          (default: auto-detected by `.harness/answers.yml` existing)
```

## Step 0 — valid git repo (blocking)

```bash
git -C <target> rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree
```

If it fails (real finding from report 08: `central-ordens-bi` was not even a git repo):
**stop and ask** the user whether you should run `git init` in the target directory, or
refuse the installation. Never proceed silently over a directory that
is not a repository — hooks/gitignore-allowlist/commits presuppose a valid git repo.

## Step 1 — deterministic scan (zero LLM)

```bash
python3 .harness/lib/scan_project.py all --target <target> > /tmp/harness-init-scan.json
```

Read the resulting JSON (`scan`, `classify`, `memory_surfaces`, `answers`).
**Do not re-derive any of these facts "by eye"** — if a piece of data you need
is not in the JSON, that is a gap in the scanner (report it, don't invent it).

## Step 2 — present the applicability report and confirm CONDICIONAL

For each component with `status: CONDICIONAL` in `classify.components`,
present it to the user in the D[n] format from the `grill-me` skill (same
principle: concrete behavior + real applied examples, never a dry "A or B?").
Use the `AskUserQuestion` tool if available in your runtime; if it is not
available (skills do not guarantee the tool — not every agent running this skill
has `AskUserQuestion`), present it as structured text and **stop, waiting
for the user's answer before proceeding** — never assume the answer.

Example block per CONDICIONAL item:

```md
### C[n] — Enable `<component>`?

**Detected signal:** <reason from JSON, verbatim>
**If YES:** <what takes effect — e.g.: "the ui-evidence-gate hook now blocks
Stop when apps/web/{app,components,styles} changes without more recent visual
evidence">
**If NO:** the component is left out of this installation (it never stays "half
enabled" — either it is in `.harness/answers.yml` as true, or it is not even asked
about in the next rounds of `copier update` until the user changes their mind).
```

Components with `status: NAO_APLICAVEL` **are not asked about** — they are
listed in the final report as "not installed (reason: <reason>)", without
asking for confirmation (asking about something that does not apply is useless friction).
`APLICAVEL` components go straight through, without a question.

Confirmations that map to real `copier.yml` fields (record the
answers, you will use them in Step 3): `has_prod_stack` (+ `prod_stack_prefix`/
`prod_registry_url`/`prod_public_web_url` if yes), `harness_understand_apps_root`
(use `understand_apps_root_offset_candidate` from the scan as a suggestion, never as
the definitive value), `has_e2e` (+ `e2e_admin_email`, never ask for the real password —
`e2e_admin_password` is `secret: true` in the schema, collect it separately/never in
plain text in the chat).

## Step 3 — assemble the final answers and run copier

Combine `answers` from the JSON (safe fields: `project_name`, `project_root`,
detected ports) with the confirmations from Step 2 and with `AGENT` from the
`ARGS:` block (`AGENT=claude` → `use_claude=true,use_codex=false`; `AGENT=codex` →
the inverse; `AGENT=both`/omitted → both `true`).

Render the WHOLE template into a **scratch** directory (never straight into the
target project — this avoids Copier's interactive conflict prompt AND allows the
controlled merge in Step 4):

```bash
uvx copier copy <path-to-orions-belt> <scratch-dir> \
  --data project_name=<...> --data owner_name=<...> \
  --data use_claude=<...> --data use_codex=<...> \
  --data has_prod_stack=<...> [other confirmed --data] \
  --defaults --trust -q
```

`<path-to-orions-belt>` is the ROOT of the repo (not `.../templates`) — since F8,
`copier.yml` lives at the root with `_subdirectory: templates` (real finding: pointing
at `templates/` directly BREAKS VCS versioning — `--vcs-ref`/tags always
ignored, "Copying from template version None"; confirmed empirically and
against `copier.readthedocs.io/en/stable/configuring/`). If you want to test against
a specific tag instead of the orions-belt HEAD, add
`--vcs-ref <tag>`.

(`MODE=update`: if `<target>/.harness/answers.yml` already exists, read it first
and reuse the values as defaults — only re-ask what changed, the same
principle as native `copier update`: "ask only what changed since the last
version".)

## Step 4 — apply into the target project: copy directly + additive merge

Two groups of files, handled differently:

1. **Framework-owned (no risk of user content)** — the entire rest of the
   rendered tree (`.harness/`, `.claude/skills/`, `.claude/hooks*` via
   `.harness/hooks/`, `.codex/`, `.agents/skills/`, hookify `*.local.md.jinja`
   rendered, `docs/SCHEMA.md`, `tasks/lessons.md` seed): copy from
   `<scratch>` into `<target>` freely (direct overwrite — these are files that
   the harness owns, not the user; if the user edited a hook manually,
   that is unsupported customization in this version of the merge, not a case to
   protect).
2. **Sensitive (may contain user content)** — `AGENTS.md`,
   `.claude/CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/settings.json`, `.gitignore`: **NEVER copy
   directly**. Always use:

   ```bash
   python3 .harness/lib/merge_docs.py markdown \
     --existing <target>/AGENTS.md --new <scratch>/AGENTS.md --label harness-init
   python3 .harness/lib/merge_docs.py markdown \
     --existing <target>/.claude/CLAUDE.md --new <scratch>/.claude/CLAUDE.md --label harness-init
   python3 .harness/lib/merge_docs.py settings-json \
     --existing <target>/.claude/settings.json --new <scratch>/.claude/settings.json
   python3 .harness/lib/merge_docs.py gitignore \
     --existing <target>/.gitignore --new <scratch>/.gitignore --label harness-init
   ```

   Each call prints `{"action": "created"|"appended"|"updated-block"|"merged", ...}`
   — report that action to the user per file (it is the proof that nothing was
   overwritten). `created` = the file did not exist, it became the pure template.
   `appended`/`updated-block` = user content preserved, the harness block
   appended/updated (Markdown and `.gitignore` use the same
   marked-block mechanism; `.gitignore` uses the `#` comment, not
   `<!-- -->`). `merged` = hooks reconciled by OWNERSHIP (A4, no longer
   naive dedup by `command`): every hook whose `command` points to
   `.harness/hooks/` is OWNED by the harness — the NEW template side always
   wins (resolves update/removal/rename of the hook itself without duplicating,
   `hooks_removed_stale_owned` in the output JSON counts how many old owned hooks
   were discarded in this round); a hook whose `command` is outside that pattern is
   EXTERNAL (the user's) and is never touched; the user's other settings.json keys
   are left untouched.

   For **non-interactive** installation (you already know the answers or are
   automating), `harness-install.sh` at the root of the orions-belt repo
   automates exactly these steps 3-4 via `--data`; this skill remains
   the path for when `CONDICIONAL` items from Step 2 need human
   confirmation in conversation — see `docs/manual/14-instalacao-e-update.md`.

## Step 4.5 — ds-gate baseline (only if `use_ds_gate` active)

H2/A6.2 (adversarial audit post-v1.0.0): `.harness/lib/ds-gate.sh` is a
RATCHET — without `.ds-baseline.txt` it always runs in `--report` (never fails,
even with obvious hardcode). `harness-install.sh` already automates this (see
the "H2/A6.2" comment in the script itself), but if you are following this
flow manually (scratch + merge, not `harness-install.sh`) and confirmed
`use_ds_gate=true` in Step 2, generate the baseline explicitly BEFORE the
final report, against the real `<target>` (never against the `<scratch>` —
scratch does not have the project's code):

```bash
[ -f "<target>/.ds-baseline.txt" ] || \
  bash <target>/.harness/lib/ds-gate.sh --update-baseline
```

Do not regenerate if `.ds-baseline.txt` already exists (reinstall/update must not
reset a ratchet in progress — regenerating is an explicit user action).
Report the generated baseline (or the already existing one) in Step 6.

## Step 5 — GLOBAL memory: only report, never write

Read `memory_surfaces.global_claude_READONLY`/`global_codex_READONLY` from the JSON
of Step 1 and **present it as information**, without action: "I found
`~/.claude/rules/context7.md` — your Context7 rule is already covered
globally, I did not duplicate it in the project's CLAUDE.md." **FORBIDDEN**: writing,
copying, or suggesting to copy any content from the `env` key of
`~/.claude/settings.json` (it may contain a plaintext secret — finding 4 of report 08). The memory
slug (`memory_surfaces.slug_computed_from`) is always the root of the target repo,
never the CWD from which you were invoked — if you were called from within a
subdirectory, the scan already corrected this via `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`.

## Step 6 — final report

Summarize: (a) APLICAVEL components installed, (b) CONDICIONAL confirmed
(enabled) vs refused (not installed), (c) NAO_APLICAVEL (not installed,
with reason), (d) action per sensitive file (created/appended/updated-block/
merged), (e) detected global surfaces (informational). Confirm that
`.harness/answers.yml` exists post-execution in the target project — it is the formal gate
of this skill.

## Known gap (not resolved in this round — future architecture decision, H2)

This flow (scratch render + direct copy + `merge_docs.py` on the 4 "sensitive"
files) is DIFFERENT from Copier's NATIVE `copier update` (a real 3-way merge,
tested end-to-end in `templates/tests/test_copier_update_e2e.sh`
— but only against a project born 100% from `copier copy`, not from a
brownfield installed by this flow). The practical difference that PERSISTS: if the
user hand-edits a "framework-owned" file outside the 4 protected ones (e.g.:
`.harness/harness.conf` — changing a port directly in the rendered file, instead
of via a copier.yml answer), Step 4 item 1 above OVERWRITES that
edit without warning ("unsupported customization in this version"). Native `copier
update`, by contrast, does a 3-way merge on ANY file (not just 4)
and generates conflict markers (`<<<<<<< before updating` / `=======` /
`>>>>>>> after updating`) instead of silently discarding — but it requires a
clean git tree at the destination, PEP440 tags on the template, and an explicit `--answers-file
.harness/answers.yml`.

(What this flow ALREADY resolves, adversarial review post-v1.0.0: B3 — our own
merge never again runs `copier copy` directly against the target project, always
scratch + merge; A2 — `core.hooksPath` never overwrites an already-configured hook
manager; A3 — a `NAO_APLICAVEL` module materializes `use_*=false` instead of
falling into the default `true`; A4 — `settings.json` reconciles by ownership of
`command`, not by naive dedup — update/removal/rename of the harness's OWN
hook are resolved correctly, only the whole file outside the 4
protected ones stays in the "direct overwrite" regime.)

Migrating `harness-init`/`harness-install.sh` to invoke native `copier update`
instead of our own merge (gaining protection on ALL files, not
just 4) is a candidate for H2 — not decided here because it would require designing the UX of
conflict resolution inside the skill (today the user would resolve it by hand in the
editor; the skill would need to guide that explicitly) AND proving
`copier update` working end-to-end over a project whose 1st
installation went through the brownfield merge (not yet tested — see
`docs/manual/14-instalacao-e-update.md`, section "Update").
